> Interesting view. (Are you counting object types or
> number of individual objects? -

Individual objects.  Admittedly, random object
generation provides more variety.

> We have 22 types of scrolls, about 15 useful scroll
> types, and about 7 very useful scroll types.

Scrolls are admittedly a fairly weak point for
monster inventory.  Mostly you get create
monster and teleport.  If in addition to
starting inventory we also count non-random
death drops, you can add scrolls of blank
paper to that (from the paper golems).
(In practice, that isn't a big deal, because
you always get enough scrolls of create
monster and teleport that you can, if you
choose, blank a large stack of them.)

This is the main reason I consider the
magic marker to be the second-most-useful
item in the game (after the wand of wishing):
the common scrolls are useful, but they
are not nearly as useful as several of the
other types of scrolls.

> We have 28 types of potions, about 15
> useful potion types, and about 7 very
> useful potion types.

Potions, on the other hand, are a real
strong point for monster inventory.  The
potions you really want as many of as
you can get are mostly healing, extra
healing, full healing, gain level, gain
ability, gain energy, and holy water.
The first four of those are common in
monster inventory (the first three are
VERY common), and the fourth (gain
ability) can be easily alchemized from
the others.  Holy water can be trivially
produced from other common potions
that you get huge stacks of from
monsters (notably object detection,
but also various uni-horn-clearables),
provided you have a co-aligned altar.
Gain energy is often in short supply,
admittedly.  See invisible is rare, but
you only need one, total, in the whole
game, or maybe a second one if you
have an unfortunate gremlin encounter.
Sickness is unimportant for most roles,
as are booze and fruit juice (in fact,
I almost always convert all three to
holy water, using a horn and amethyst
to reduce the number of stacks).
Unholy water is rare in the extreme,
but you only need *one*, plus easily
produced uncursed water, to make
as much as you need.  Restore ability
is rare, but if you have a unicorn horn
that doesn't really matter.  Monster
detection is rare, but you can eat a
floating eye and use a blindfold to
get most of its benefit.  Enlightenment
is rare, but you don't need it often.
(I usually hope to find *one* wand
of enlightenment and make it last
the whole game.)  Levitation is rare,
but the potion isn't the source of lev
you really want anyway.  Polymorph
and oil are rare, but that's clearly by
design.

> We have 28 types of rings, about
> 20 useful ring types, and about 7
> very useful ring types.

Rings are fundamentally different
from potions and scrolls, because
you only need *one* of each kind.
Thus, the fact that they are for the
most part not common is largely
unimportant.

> We have 24 types of wands, about
> 17 useful wand types, and about 7
> very useful wand types.

There's a lot of overlap between useful
wands and wands that are commonly
found in monster inventory.  Most of
the attack wands, including death, are
found more often from monster starting
inventory than from any other source.
The wand of digging, arguably the
second-most-useful non-attack wand
(after wishing, which is rare by design)
is fantastically abundant in monster
starting inventory.  Also teleport and
create monster.

Admittedly, you don't get wands of
cancellation or polymorph from monster
inventory, and those are both useful.

> And we have a lot more useful and
> very useful items from the book object
> class, from the tools, and more.

None of which are particularly common,
either in monster inventory or otherwise.
Which is a pattern:  things that are not
common in monster inventory tend not
to be common in general.

> In my games I typically use much more
> types of useful scrolls from random
> sources than from monster inventory.

More types, yes, but fewer total scrolls --
and scrolls are, as I said, a particular
weak point for monster inventory.

